WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.835
Attention is a weighted average.

2
00:00:02.115 --> 00:00:07.811
The lecture builds up to self-attention in steps that are worth replaying in mathematical terms.

3
00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:12.059
Version 1 averages all previous token vectors with loops.

4
00:00:12.339 --> 00:00:27.592
Version 2 notices that a running average is a matrix multiply: multiply the (T, C) token matrix by a lower-triangular (T, T) matrix whose row t holds 1/(t + 1) in its first t + 1 entries.

5
00:00:27.872 --> 00:00:36.896
Version 3 gets the same matrix by putting zeros on and below the diagonal, minus infinity above it, and applying a softmax to each row.

6
00:00:37.176 --> 00:00:41.635
Version 4 replaces those zeros with scores computed from the data.

7
00:00:41.915 --> 00:00:51.344
So each output y_t is a convex combination of value vectors v_s for s at most t: the weights are nonnegative and sum to 1.

8
00:00:51.624 --> 00:00:55.912
Geometrically, y_t lies in the convex hull of the past values.

9
00:00:56.192 --> 00:00:58.603
What the network learns is where to put the weight.

10
00:00:58.883 --> 00:01:11.149
Each position emits a query (“what am I looking for”), a key (“what do I contain”), and a value (“what I’ll pass on if chosen”); the weight on s is a softmax of how well t’s query matches s’s key.

11
00:01:11.429 --> 00:01:22.992
The lecture’s first note at 01:11:38 calls this communication: tokens are nodes in a directed graph, each aggregating information from the nodes that point to it.

12
00:01:23.272 --> 00:01:28.221
With the causal mask, the graph has an edge from every earlier position to every later one.

13
00:01:28.501 --> 00:01:35.136
The third note adds that no communication happens across the batch dimension: each sequence is its own graph.
